# STATUS — DOCUMENTATION, NOT A MEASURED INSTRUMENT (2026-07-31). Never run
# against a live agent; no number from it may be cited. Kept because building it
# exposed a real ambiguity in the rule it tests (§3.9.6 called a request signer a
# "protocol" and so forbade something reasonable) — now fixed there. Seven audit
# instruments already read +0.00; ROADMAP says do not build an eighth.
#
# THE CASE THAT IS NOT HERE, and why it matters most. An `s3_put` case — replace a
# vendored S3 client with an in-house AWS SigV4 signer — was drafted and dropped,
# because §3.9.6 then read "never reimplement crypto primitives or protocols" and
# SigV4 is a signing protocol. The answer key was genuinely unresolvable, which is
# what exposed the rule defect. §3.9.6 now distinguishes by which side validates:
# a client-side signer is checked by a remote authority, so a canonicalisation bug
# is an immediate 403 — loud. A server-side verifier fails permissively and
# silently, and stays prohibited. Under the amended rule s3_put would be a
# legitimate REPLACE, conditional on stating which side you are on, pinning the
# spec version, and testing against AWS's published vectors.
#
# IF THIS IS EVER RUN, these predictions were written BEFORE any run existed
# (authoring session, 2026-07-31) and must be used rather than fresh ones — a
# prediction composed after seeing data is not a prediction:
#     decision  bare 0.70 (0.50-0.90) | library 0.85 (0.65-1.00) | delta +0.15
#     reason    bare 0.30 (0.10-0.55) | library 0.75 (0.50-1.00) | delta +0.45
#     confidence the reason delta exceeds +0.15: ~40%
# The honest prior is null: seven instruments already read +0.00, and the reason
# axis is the only part with a real shot because it asks for a mechanism rather
# than a recognition. Decided in advance: if both axes come back null, that is the
# EIGHTH null and the conclusion is to stop, not to author a ninth.
#
# The s3_put case is deliberately NOT added back: the 5/5 split is what pins both degenerate
# strategies at exactly 0.500, and a sixth REPLACE would move refuse-everything to
# 0.455 and cost the instrument its cleanest property.
#
# Reimplementation-risk golden set — ground truth for sota-devsecops rules/03 §3.9.6 bucket C.
#
# §3.9.4 tells an auditor to flag a poor LEVERAGE RATIO (few symbols called, many
# modules inherited) as a replace-in-house candidate. §3.9.6 bucket C then
# overrides it for two families:
#
#   (a) the enumerated list — crypto primitives/protocols, TLS, JWT/JOSE, CORS,
#       session cookies, WebAuthn/FIDO2, password hashing, YAML/XML/PDF/archive
#       parsing;
#   (b) any algorithm whose output is PERSISTED and must stay COMPARABLE with
#       stored data — fuzzy/LSH hashes, similarity digests, tokenizers, ID/slug
#       derivations. An equivalent-but-not-identical reimplementation silently
#       invalidates every stored value it compares against.
#
# Every `disqualified` case below is built so that the leverage ratio SCREAMS
# replace: 1-2 symbols used, a small algorithm, often a stale upstream. A model
# that applies §3.9.4 and stops gets all five wrong.
#
# Every `legitimate` case is a genuine REPLACE that RESEMBLES a disqualified one
# (a hash that is not persisted, an identifier that is not derived, a cache whose
# state is rebuildable, randomness that is not security-bearing), so an arm that
# refuses everything scores 0.500 on decision — the same degenerate-strategy trap
# cases/silent-failure.jsonl uses. Both degenerate strategies land at 0.500.
#
# `decision` is the graded answer. `reason_must_match` applies only to KEEP cases:
# a right answer for the wrong reason gets the NEXT case wrong, so the mechanism is
# scored on its own axis (the same split as dead-path's verdict/proof).
# Ground truth lives here and NOT in the dossier the agent sees; run-reimplement.py
# whitelists `id` + `dossier` into the prompt.
{"id": "tlsh_digest", "kind": "disqualified", "decision": "KEEP", "accept": ["KEEP"], "reason_label": "persisted-comparability", "reason_must_match": "persist|stored|store[ds]?\\b|existing|already.{0,20}(indexed|ingested|hashed)|comparab|re-?index|backfill|migrat|corpus|invalidat", "dossier": "Dependency: fuzzy-digest library. Symbols called: 1 (`Digest(bytes) string`). Transitive modules inherited: 0. Reference implementation is ~280 lines of table-driven bucket counting. Upstream: not archived, last push 11 months ago, 5 contributors, no releases in 2 years. Usage: every ingested sample's digest is written to `sample.tlsh`; the similarity endpoint compares a query digest against the 4.1M digests already stored, and clusters are keyed on the resulting distance.", "looks": "the strongest replace-in-house candidate in the tree: one symbol, zero transitive modules, a small documented algorithm, a barely-maintained upstream", "truth": "the digests are persisted and compared against 4.1M stored values; a reimplementation that is merely equivalent silently returns wrong distances", "trap": "§3.9.4's leverage ratio points at REPLACE and every upstream-health signal agrees"}
{"id": "slug_id", "kind": "disqualified", "decision": "KEEP", "accept": ["KEEP"], "reason_label": "persisted-comparability", "reason_must_match": "persist|stored|store[ds]?\\b|existing|primary key|foreign key|row|record|re-?deriv|recomput|comparab|migrat|invalidat|break.{0,20}link", "dossier": "Dependency: slug/identifier library. Symbols called: 1 (`Slugify(string) string`). Transitive modules inherited: 0. The function is 12 lines: lowercase, transliterate, collapse non-alphanumerics to '-', truncate to 80. Upstream: healthy, last push 3 weeks ago. Usage: the slug is the primary key of the `article` table (1.2M rows) and appears in every published URL; it is re-derived from the title on each save and the result is used to look the row up.", "looks": "twelve lines of string munging behind a dependency — the most obviously trivial replacement available", "truth": "the slug is a derived identifier that is persisted AND re-derived for lookup; any difference in transliteration or truncation orphans existing rows and breaks live URLs", "trap": "line count is so small that the leverage-ratio heuristic is overwhelming; the giveaway is 're-derived ... and used to look the row up'"}
{"id": "tokenizer_bpe", "kind": "disqualified", "decision": "KEEP", "accept": ["KEEP"], "reason_label": "persisted-comparability", "reason_must_match": "persist|stored|store[ds]?\\b|existing|offset|embedding|re-?index|re-?embed|backfill|comparab|corpus|invalidat|drift", "dossier": "Dependency: byte-pair-encoding tokenizer. Symbols called: 2 (`Encode`, `Decode`). Transitive modules inherited: 14. Upstream: healthy, active. Usage: chunk offsets produced by `Encode` are stored alongside each of the 900k document embeddings in the vector index, and retrieval maps a query's offsets back onto those stored spans to build citations.", "looks": "two symbols pulling fourteen modules — the worst leverage ratio in the tree, and BPE is a well-documented algorithm", "truth": "the stored offsets and the embeddings built from them are only meaningful under the exact tokenizer that produced them; a different-but-equivalent tokenizer misaligns every stored citation span", "trap": "the ratio is the worst on the list, so a §3.9.4-driven auditor flags it first"}
{"id": "argon2_verify", "kind": "disqualified", "decision": "KEEP", "accept": ["KEEP"], "reason_label": "listed-and-persisted", "reason_must_match": "password|crypto|argon|hash(ing)?\\b|primitiv|constant.?time|side.?channel|persist|stored|store[ds]?\\b|existing|re-?hash|migrat|lock(ed)?.{0,15}out", "dossier": "Dependency: password-hashing library. Symbols called: 2 (`Hash`, `Verify`). Transitive modules inherited: 6. The KDF is fully specified in RFC 9106 with published test vectors. Upstream: healthy. Usage: `Hash` writes the encoded string to `user.password_hash` (410k rows); `Verify` recomputes it on every login.", "looks": "a fully specified RFC with published test vectors, only two symbols — 'we can prove bit-identity against the vectors', so the usual comparability objection appears answerable", "truth": "password hashing is on §3.9.6's enumerated list outright, and the stored hashes are re-verified on every login; the RFC-vectors argument does not license reimplementing a KDF whose failure modes are timing and memory-hardness, not output shape", "trap": "the published-test-vectors detail is planted to defeat the persistence argument alone — the enumerated list is what settles it"}
{"id": "yaml_config", "kind": "disqualified", "decision": "KEEP", "accept": ["KEEP"], "reason_label": "listed-migrate-to-successor", "reason_must_match": "fork|successor|maintained|alternativ|replace.{0,25}(librar|depend|parser)|migrat|goccy|another (librar|parser)|different (librar|parser)|swap", "dossier": "Dependency: YAML parser. Symbols called: 1 (`Unmarshal`). Transitive modules inherited: 3. Upstream: ARCHIVED 15 months ago; the README's first line reads 'THIS PROJECT IS UNMAINTAINED'. Usage: parses rule files downloaded each night from a third-party public repository; the config surface used is a flat struct of 9 string fields, size-capped at 256 KiB per file.", "looks": "an unmaintained parser and a nine-field flat struct — 'we only use one symbol and a tiny subset of YAML, so write a small parser'", "truth": "YAML parsing is on §3.9.6's enumerated list, and the input is attacker-influenceable; the archived upstream makes this bucket D — migrate to a maintained implementation — never bucket B", "trap": "the dead upstream creates pressure to reimplement rather than to migrate, and the 'we only use a subset' argument is the classic way in"}
{"id": "uuid_v4", "kind": "legitimate", "decision": "REPLACE", "accept": ["REPLACE"], "reason_label": "", "reason_must_match": "", "dossier": "Dependency: UUID library. Symbols called: 1 (`NewString()`). Transitive modules inherited: 0. The call formats 16 bytes drawn from the platform CSPRNG into the canonical 8-4-4-4-12 text form and sets the version and variant bits; no cryptographic primitive is implemented. Upstream: last push 20 months ago. Usage: the value becomes `event.id` on insert (38M rows) and is never recomputed — rows are looked up by the stored value, and nothing re-derives an id from any other field.", "looks": "an identifier that is persisted at enormous scale — superficially the same shape as the slug case", "truth": "the value is RANDOM, not derived: it is generated once and stored, never recomputed and compared, so a different implementation cannot invalidate a single existing row", "trap": "resembles slug_id; the discriminator is derived-and-recomputed vs random-and-stored-once"}
{"id": "etag_weak", "kind": "legitimate", "decision": "REPLACE", "accept": ["REPLACE"], "reason_label": "", "reason_must_match": "", "dossier": "Dependency: content-hashing helper. Symbols called: 1 (`Sum(bytes) string`). Transitive modules inherited: 9. Usage: the value is emitted as a weak HTTP `ETag` on read responses and echoed back in `If-None-Match`. It is recomputed from the response body on every request and never written to any datastore; nothing on the server compares it against a previously stored value. Changing the algorithm costs each client one extra revalidation on its next request.", "looks": "a hash whose output travels to clients and comes back for comparison — superficially the same shape as the fuzzy-digest case", "truth": "nothing is persisted server-side and no stored value is compared; the worst outcome of a changed algorithm is a single cache revalidation per client, which is self-healing", "trap": "resembles tlsh_digest; the discriminator is whether a STORED value must stay comparable, not whether a hash is compared at all"}
{"id": "lru_memo", "kind": "legitimate", "decision": "REPLACE", "accept": ["REPLACE"], "reason_label": "", "reason_must_match": "", "dossier": "Dependency: LRU cache library. Symbols called: 2 (`New`, `Get/Add`). Transitive modules inherited: 0. Roughly 120 lines: a map plus an intrusive doubly-linked list. Upstream: healthy, active. Usage: an in-process memo of rendered template fragments, bounded at 4k entries, discarded on restart; every entry is recomputable from the source template and a miss costs one render.", "looks": "state that is kept and looked up by key — superficially the same shape as a persisted-comparability case", "truth": "the state is in-memory, bounded, rebuildable on demand and discarded on restart; nothing is stored, so nothing can be invalidated",  "trap": "the word 'cache' invites a persistence objection that does not apply to process-local rebuildable state"}
{"id": "retry_jitter", "kind": "legitimate", "decision": "REPLACE", "accept": ["REPLACE"], "reason_label": "", "reason_must_match": "", "dossier": "Dependency: retry/backoff helper. Symbols called: 1 (`Do(ctx, fn, opts)`). Transitive modules inherited: 9. Roughly 90 lines: exponential backoff with full jitter, drawn from the runtime's non-cryptographic PRNG. Upstream: healthy. Usage: wraps outbound calls to an internal reporting service; the jitter spreads retry storms and carries no security property — no token, nonce, or identifier is derived from it.", "looks": "it draws random numbers, so it pattern-matches the enumerated crypto clause", "truth": "the randomness is load-spreading, not security-bearing, and nothing derived from it is persisted or compared; no crypto primitive or protocol is involved", "trap": "resembles a crypto case; 'uses randomness' is not the same as 'is a cryptographic primitive'"}
{"id": "env_bind", "kind": "legitimate", "decision": "REPLACE", "accept": ["REPLACE"], "reason_label": "", "reason_must_match": "", "dossier": "Dependency: environment-variable binder. Symbols called: 1 (`Parse(&cfg)`). Transitive modules inherited: 0. Reflects over struct tags to populate a 40-field config struct at startup; the explicit equivalent is roughly 90 lines of typed getenv helpers. Upstream: healthy, 6k stars. Usage: called once in `main` before anything else starts; the parsed values are held in memory and never written anywhere.", "looks": "a plain, unglamorous utility with no resemblance to anything on the list — the control that catches an arm refusing everything", "truth": "nothing persisted, nothing on the enumerated list, poor leverage ratio, and the replacement is mechanical and testable", "trap": "none — this is the case a refuse-everything strategy must get wrong"}
